--TEST--
`Core\Test::assertCompletes` asks for the clock before it calls `$body`, so a refused call runs nothing at all -- `rule:testing/task-tree-and-virtual-clock`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The ordering, asserted by what did *not* print. A member that ran the body
// first and only then found it had no clock would answer the same `LogicError`
// on the same line while having executed the caller's work -- and the work is
// the half a test cannot take back. The budget has to be granted before the
// body measures itself against it, so "no clock" has to be answered before the
// call, and that is only observable from the outside as silence.
//
// The three bodies each echo, so a single one of them running turns this case
// red; the trailing counter is what makes the silence a claim about the member
// rather than an empty `--EXPECT--` nobody would notice going stale.

use Core\Test;

int $refused = 0;

try {
    Core\Test::assertCompletes(fn (): void => { echo "first body\n"; }, {within: 0s});
} catch (LogicError $one) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}

try {
    Core\Test::assertCompletes(fn (): void => { echo "second body\n"; }, {within: 50ms});
} catch (LogicError $two) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}

try {
    Core\Test::assertCompletes(fn (): void => { echo "third body\n"; }, {within: 2h}, {message: "drain"});
} catch (LogicError $three) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}

echo "refused=", $refused, "/3\n";
echo "bodies that ran: none of them\n";
--EXPECT--
refused=3/3
bodies that ran: none of them
